OpenStax's source MathML for exercise 27 (numbered 80 in OpenStax's own
scheme) wraps the "y" in "y_p(x)" as <mtext>y</mtext> instead of <mi>y</mi>
-- every other occurrence of y_p on this page (all ~70 of them, including
the three sibling exercises 28-30 in the same "Find the unique solution..."
group) uses the proper <mi>y</mi><mi>p</mi> structure. The converter
faithfully reproduces the source's own mistake as $\text{y}_{p}(x)$ (upright
roman y), which renders inconsistently with every other $y_p(x)$ on the
page. Fixed by hand here rather than in the converter, since the source
data is simply wrong for this one exercise.

